maia-kmod: invalidate rxbuffer caches through a kernel mapping

IOCTL_CACHEINV invalidated L1 through the userspace address of the
buffer mapping, saved in a driver-global vm_start. 0002-0004 guard that
address with mmap_done and a mutex, but cannot close the gap: munmap()
tears down the page tables before it calls vm_ops->close, so an ioctl
that already passed the mmap_done check can still run DC IMVAC on an
address with no mapping. On ARMv7 such a cache operation can abort,
and v7_dma_inv_range() has no exception fixup (unlike the USER()
wrapped v7_coherent_user_range), so the result is a kernel oops. The
address is also only meaningful in the process that created the
mapping.

Map the reserved rxbuffer region into the kernel at probe time with
devm_memremap(MEMREMAP_WB) and invalidate through that mapping. The
Cortex-A9 L1 data cache is physically indexed and tagged, so this
removes the lines userspace reads through its own mapping. The kernel
mapping lives as long as the device, so the invalidation no longer
depends on any userspace mapping and needs no lock. vm_start goes away;
mmap_done and the mutex stay to keep the one-mapping rule.

Also invalidate the outer (L2) cache before L1, in both the rxbuffer
and the recording path. That is the order Linux uses after a
DMA_FROM_DEVICE transfer; with L1 first, a speculative L1 refill
between the two operations can bring stale L2 data back into L1.

diff --git a/maia-kmod/maia-sdr.c b/maia-kmod/maia-sdr.c
index dbb6797..a13f1f5 100644
